#-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# fpga/targets/@BOARD@/@BLOCK@.drc.xdc  --  CONFIGURATION, AND WHICH RULES THIS
#                                           DESIGN IS ALLOWED TO BREAK
#
# Scaffolded into @PROJECT@ by `fpga-flow-init` on @DATE@.
# Named by XDC_DRC in fpga/design.mk. READ IN IMPLEMENTATION ONLY.
#
# WHAT BELONGS IN THIS FILE
#   Configuration properties of the implemented design, and DRC SEVERITY
#   changes - the rules this design is knowingly, deliberately, in writing,
#   allowed to break.
#
# WHAT DOES NOT
#   * pins and IO standards  ->  @BLOCK@.pins.xdc
#   * clocks and exceptions  ->  @BLOCK@.timing.xdc
#   * A WAIVER. Read the trap below: a waiver cannot live in an XDC at all.
#
# ── WHY THIS FILE IS IMPLEMENTATION-ONLY ────────────────────────────────────
#
# Nothing in it means anything to synthesis. A DRC severity applies to checks
# that run on a placed and routed design; a configuration property describes
# how the device is loaded. Read during synthesis they would apply to a design
# state that has none of those things - and since Vivado drops a constraint
# that matches nothing without an error, that would be silence, not a warning.
#
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# TRAP: A WAIVER CANNOT BE read_xdc'd. AT ALL.
#
#   `create_waiver` is PROCEDURAL TCL, and Vivado REJECTS procedural Tcl inside
#   a constraints file. So the file you would naturally write - one waiver per
#   finding, each with its diagnosis - is not a constraint file, and read_xdc
#   on it fails in a way that reads like a syntax problem in your waiver.
#
#   A waiver goes in XDC_POST_ROUTE, which the flow `source`s as ordinary Tcl
#   AFTER route_design. That is where it has to go anyway: a waiver waives a
#   FINDING, and the findings do not exist until the design is routed.
#
#       # in design.mk
#       XDC_POST_ROUTE ?= $(TARGET_DIR)/$(BLOCK).waivers.tcl
#
#   Do not use that seam for anything that changes the design. By the time it
#   runs, place and route are done.
#
#   THE DIFFERENCE BETWEEN THE TWO MECHANISMS IS NOT COSMETIC:
#     set_property SEVERITY   changes how a rule is REPORTED, everywhere, for
#                             every object, for the life of the file
#     create_waiver           excuses ONE FINDING, on named objects, with a
#                             description and an owner recorded in the report
#   Prefer the waiver. A severity downgrade is a blanket, and the next
#   violation of that rule - the real one - arrives already silenced.
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# ── THE RULE FOR EVERYTHING IN THIS FILE ────────────────────────────────────
#
# EVERY ENTRY CARRIES A PARAGRAPH OF DIAGNOSIS AND A NAMED OWNER, beside it,
# here, in this file. That is the same rule MSG_GATE_ALLOWLIST follows in
# design.mk and it exists because of the same failure: a list of exemptions
# nobody can date, attribute or re-derive becomes a list nobody dares remove,
# and a gate that carries it passes on the defect it was written to catch.
#
# An entry with no diagnosis is not a decision, it is a residue.
#
# ── EVERY .xdc HERE MUST BE NAMED BY A VARIABLE IN design.mk ────────────────
#
# An .xdc in this directory that no variable names is an ERROR from
# `make check`, deliberately: the flow this replaces discovered constraints by
# a glob and silently ignored anything named differently.
#
# ── IF THIS DESIGN NEEDS NOTHING HERE ───────────────────────────────────────
#
# Delete the marked lines below and leave the file. It is legitimately empty of
# constraints on a first build, and `make check` warns about an all-comments
# file only for XDC_PINS - where empty means "unconstrained pins" - and not for
# this one, where empty means "no rule is being bent".
#
# Copyright (C) 2026, SoC Labs (www.soclabs.org)
#-----------------------------------------------------------------------------


#### 1. CONFIGURATION PROPERTIES ###############################################
#
# BEFORE YOU SET ONE HERE: check whether the PART PACK already declares it.
# `cfgbvs`, `config_voltage` and `bitstream_compress` are part-pack keys -
# facts about the device and how it is configured, which every project on that
# device wants the same answer to - and `make part-probe` prints what the pack
# resolved without launching a tool.
#
# Setting one in both places gives one fact two sources, and the one that wins
# is whichever is applied last. If the pack is wrong, fix the pack; if this
# board needs something different from every other board with that device, set
# it here and write down which board fact makes it different.
#
# set_property CFGBVS         <VCCO|GND>  [current_design]
# set_property CONFIG_VOLTAGE <volts>     [current_design]
#
# Bitstream properties are properties of the implemented design too. Compression
# changes the SIZE of the .bit and nothing else about the design - and the .bin
# conversion that follows it is board-family dependent (BIN_STYLE: zynq7 needs
# a byte swap, zynqmp a header strip, and the wrong one corrupts a load that
# reports no error).
#
# set_property BITSTREAM.GENERAL.COMPRESS <TRUE|FALSE> [current_design]


#### 2. DRC SEVERITY CHANGES ###################################################
#
# One block per rule. Fill in the markers or DELETE THIS BLOCK - an
# undiagnosed exemption is worse than no file, and a scaffold that shipped one
# already downgraded would be handing every new project somebody else's
# undiagnosed exemption, which is exactly how a gate comes to pass on the
# defect it was written to catch.
#
# WHAT TO WRITE IN THE MARKERS
#   rule    the DRC check id, exactly as the implementation report prints it
#   why     what the tool is objecting to, why it is not true of this design,
#           and how that was established - the run, the date, the report
#   owner   a person. Not a team, not "TBD"
#
# rule:  <<FILL IN: the DRC rule id, from the first implementation run's report>>
# why:   <<FILL IN: the diagnosis - what the rule objects to and why it is wrong here>>
# owner: <<FILL IN: who owns this decision>>
# set_property SEVERITY {Warning} [get_drc_checks <the rule id above>]
#
# A rule id that does not exist matches nothing and reports nothing, so a typo
# here is a downgrade that never happened AND a rule you believe is silenced.
# `get_drc_checks` with no argument lists them in a Vivado shell:
#     make vivado-shell


#### 3. WHAT THE FLOW ALREADY GATES, SO YOU DO NOT REPEAT IT HERE ##############
#
# EXPECT_UNROUTED_MAX and EXPECT_BLACKBOX_MAX default to ZERO in design.mk, not
# to -1, because unlike a resource budget there is no design for which the
# right answer is "some": an unrouted net is not a tight number, it is a
# bitstream that does not implement the schematic, and a black box is a module
# the tools never read.
#
# ALLOW_CRITICAL_WARNINGS defaults to 0 for the same reason. Do not downgrade a
# critical warning here to get a build through - the flow's message gate is
# where that decision is recorded, with its diagnosis and its owner, and one
# place to look is worth more than two ways to do it.

